│ PENTESTAGENT - Non-interactive Mode                                                                │
│                                                                                                    │
│ Target: http://127.0.0.1:5000                                                                      │
│ Model: openai/qwen3.6-27b-fable                                                                    │
│ Mode: Agent                                                                                        │
│ Runtime: Local                                                                                     │
│ Max loops: 45                                                                                      │
│ Task: Final gate before delivery. Confirms every finding recorded earlier either survives or is    │
│ explicitly accounted for, and enforces a mechanical evidence standard: control results present,    │
│ quoted secrets re-read and length-checked, algorithm claims citing source lines, execution claims  │
│ showing returned output. Emits the final report.                                                   │
│                                                                                                    │
│ Phase: Completeness check                                                                          │
│ Objective: Nothing that was found earlier may vanish silently between phases.                      │
│ Techniques:                                                                                        │
│   1. START FROM THE VERDICT RECORD. Read `adversarial_verdicts.md` in this assessment's directory, │
│ plus the notes. Those two are your primary input and together they carry the verdicts, the quoted  │
│ evidence and the scope for every claim that survived review.                                       │
│   2. CHECK THE VERDICT RECORD IS ACTUALLY POPULATED before relying on it. If it is missing, or     │
│ holds only a header with no verdicts, the review phase did not finish and you have no structured   │
│ input. Say so plainly in your output and read the per-phase reports directly instead. An empty     │
│ primary input is not a reason to report less; it is a reason to use the secondary one and record   │
│ that the pipeline lost a step.                                                                     │
│   3. DO NOT RE-READ EVERY PER-PHASE REPORT OR PHASE LOG AS A MATTER OF COURSE. Consolidation and   │
│ review have each already swept them; a third sweep is the single largest waste of this phase's     │
│ budget and it is why previous runs stopped before finishing. Open a specific report or log only to │
│ resolve a NAMED gap - a finding the verdict record mentions without evidence, or a note with no    │
│ corresponding verdict - and say which gap you are chasing.                                         │
│   4. Compare them. Every finding present before the chaining and review phases must appear in the  │
│ reviewed set, be marked REFUTED with evidence, or be explicitly listed as dropped with the reason. │
│ Silent disappearance is not acceptable.                                                            │
│   5. Check the notes for low-severity observations that never reached a verdict - missing security │
│ headers, permissive cross-origin policy, verbose errors and stack traces, version disclosure.      │
│ These vanish because each is individually minor and nobody carries them forward. The notes already │
│ hold them, so this is a comparison against the verdict record, not another trawl through the logs. │
│   6. Check that any material recovered during the assessment was actually applied to something. A  │
│ secret, token or credential that was recovered and never used is either an untested lead that must │
│ be labelled as such, or a missed critical finding.                                                 │
│                                                                                                    │
│ Phase: Evidence integrity check                                                                    │
│ Objective: Apply a mechanical standard to every surviving finding. These are checks, not           │
│ judgements - each either passes or does not.                                                       │
│ Techniques:                                                                                        │
│   1. CONTROL RESULT PRESENT - every finding claiming access, bypass or authentication must record  │
│ the result of the same request made WITHOUT the credential, token, header or material said to be   │
│ responsible. If that result is missing, obtain it now. If it matches the claimed result, the       │
│ finding is REFUTED: the resource was already reachable.                                            │
│   2. QUOTED VALUES RE-READ AND LENGTH-CHECKED - for every secret, key or token quoted in the       │
│ report, re-read it from the source artifact rather than from the draft, and assert its length is   │
│ one the stated algorithm accepts. A value quoted at an impossible length is a transcription error: │
│ correct it from the artifact. Check the quoted length claim matches the value actually printed.    │
│   3. ALGORITHM CLAIMS CITE SOURCE - any statement about a cipher, mode, padding, hash or key       │
│ derivation must cite the line of recovered source that sets it. Do not accept an inferred mode;    │
│ read it. Being right about the vulnerability and wrong about the mechanism produces incorrect      │
│ remediation advice.                                                                                │
│   4. EXECUTION CLAIMS SHOW RETURNED OUTPUT - any claimed code or command execution must show       │
│ output of a command chosen by the tester, returned by the target. A payload that was sent, or an   │
│ error that looks interesting, is not execution. Downgrade otherwise.                               │
│   5. SEVERITY MATCHES DEMONSTRATION - severity is set by what was DEMONSTRATED, not by what is     │
│ suspected. State breadth explicitly: one object or all of them, one account or every account. An   │
│ unproven critical theory is a lead, not a critical finding.                                        │
│   6. Correct anything that fails a check rather than deleting it - downgrade the proof level, fix  │
│ the transcription, or obtain the missing evidence, and say what was corrected.                     │
│                                                                                                    │
│ Phase: Emit the final deliverable                                                                  │
│ Objective: One report, honest about proof and about coverage.                                      │
│ Techniques:                                                                                        │
│   1. Produce the final report with every finding carrying CONFIRMED, REFUTED or UNPROVEN, its      │
│ evidence, and its control result.                                                                  │
│   2. QUOTE THE EVIDENCE, NEVER CITE IT. For every finding marked DEMONSTRATED or CONFIRMED, paste  │
│ the actual artefact into the report: the request that was sent, the response that came back, and   │
│ any value that was recovered. A filename, a note name or a phase name is a pointer, not evidence - │
│ the reader cannot open your working directory, and a pointer proves nothing.                       │
│   3. If you cannot find and quote the concrete artefact for a finding, you may not mark it         │
│ DEMONSTRATED. Downgrade it to UNPROVEN and say what is missing. This applies however confident an  │
│ earlier phase sounded: a phase asserting something is not the same as a phase showing it.          │
│   4. CLAIM EXACTLY WHAT THE EVIDENCE SHOWS AND NOT ONE STEP MORE. The most common failure in this  │
│ report is a demonstrated step being written up as the consequence it implies. Recovering a         │
│ plaintext credential is not the same as authenticating with it; reaching an endpoint is not the    │
│ same as extracting data from it; decrypting a token is not the same as taking over an account.     │
│ Each of those is a separate step needing its own request and its own response.                     │
│   5. Before writing any impact sentence, find the quoted evidence that supports it. If the         │
│ evidence shows a decrypt but no authenticated response, the finding is 'credential recovered', and │
│ the takeover is stated as the untested consequence it is. Overstating one finding discredits the   │
│ entire report.                                                                                     │
│   6. Order findings by demonstrated severity, and state impact concretely - what data or           │
│ capability was obtained and across how many accounts or objects.                                   │
│   7. EVERY FINDING CARRIES TWO REQUIRED SECTIONS, taken from the verdict record. COMPOUNDING       │
│ FACTORS: which other findings make this worse and what the combination yields, quoting any         │
│ recovered value that does the unlocking. MITIGATING FACTORS: what this finding does not show - the │
│ next step not attempted, the precondition still needed, the control that limits it.                │
│   8. Write 'None identified' rather than omitting a section. A section left out cannot be told     │
│ apart from a question never asked, and these two are where an assessment is most often wrong in    │
│ both directions - understating by leaving a chain implicit, overstating by folding an untested     │
│ step into the impact sentence.                                                                     │
│   9. The compounding section is where a chain belongs. If material recovered in one finding was    │
│ applied to a surface described in another, both findings say so and both quote the recovered       │
│ value. A chain mentioned in neither is a chain the reader will not see.                            │
│   10. Report chains as ordered steps with the request and response at each link, so a reader can   │
│ follow and reproduce them.                                                                         │
│   11. State coverage honestly: which categories were assessed, which produced no findings, and     │
│ which could not be assessed from outside. A category with nothing found is reported as such, not   │
│ omitted.                                                                                           │
│   12. List explicitly what was corrected or dropped during this gate and why, so the reader can    │
│ see the report was checked rather than assembled.                                                  │
│   13. Name the root cause of each finding precisely - file, type and member for embedded material; │
│ endpoint and parameter for exposure - so each is separately fixable.                               │
│                                                                                                    │
│                                                                                                    │
